Gas sensors can produce qualitative or quantitative predictions about the composition of a gas. Qualitative gas sensors aim to discriminate between distinctive and, often, complex gas mixtures, for example between the breath of healthy patients and of lung cancer patients.12 Quantitative gas sensors, the subject of this article, aim to determine the concentrations of components in a gas mixture, for example of carbon monoxide in the air of a home.13
Nanoporous materials (NPMs), such as metal−organic frameworks (MOFs),14 covalent organic frameworks,15 porous organic cage molecules,16 and metal−organic polyhedra,17 could be utilized as recognition elements in gas sensors by exploiting their selective gas adsorption properties.18−24 In principle, any measurable property of a NPM that is altered by the adsorption of gassuch as mass,25 luminescence, 26 mechanical strain,27,28 color,31 or electrical properties29 such as conductivity30 could be exploited to infer changes in the gas composition. Without much loss of generalization, we focus on utilizing a MOF as a sensor by monitoring the mass of adsorbed gas in it.19 The mass of adsorbed gas depends upon and thus contains information about the composition of the bulk gas in which the MOF is immersed; given a mathematical model of gas adsorption in the MOF, observation of the adsorbed mass of gas in the MOF places a constraint on the composition of the gas phase. A mass-based MOF sensor can be miniaturized by depositing a thin $\mathrm{{flm}}^{32-34}$ of the MOF on a quartz crystal microbalance $(\mathrm{QCM})^{35}$ or a surface acoustic wave (SAW) device,36 which can measure the (total) mass of gas adsorbed in the thin film at the nano-gram scale.19 Several experimental feasibility studies demonstrated gas sensing with MOF-coated $\mathrm{QCMs}^{2\dot{5},37-44}$ and $\mathrm{SAWs^{45-47}}$ for binary gas mixtures or distinct, pure gases.
Engineering MOFs that diversely interact with the different components of the gas is paramount for the development of MOF-based sensors. Attributed to their modular synthesis (building blocks: metal nodes/clusters and organic linker molecules)48 and postsynthetic modifiability,49 many MOFs with diverse pore sizes, shapes, and surface chemistries, and therefore with diverse interactions with gases, can be and have been synthesized.50
Any individual mass-based MOF sensor, however, is insufficient for quantitative gas sensing in domains where the gas mixture has multiple degrees of freedom (e.g., gas mixtures whose components vary independently in concentration) because of cross-sensitivity. Practically, many components of a gas mixture will adsorb in the MOF and contribute to the adsorbed mass (although, to varying extents). Consequently, any given (total) adsorbed mass in the MOF could result from many different compositions, rendering the single-sensor response $\bf{\sigma}\to\bf{g a s}$ composition problem noninvertible.
MOF-based gas sensor arrays51 confront cross-sensitivity by using measurements of the adsorbed mass of gas in multiple, distinct MOFs to infer the gas composition (see Figure 1).
Despite that each MOF in the array could adsorb all components of the gas mixture (cross-sensitivity), the collection of adsorbed mass measurements in each MOF in the array (the response pattern) could provide sufficient information to determine the composition of a multicomponent mixture, that is, to render the sensor array response pattern $\rightarrow$ gas composition problem invertible. In other words, a cross-sensitive gas sensor array is capable of producing an information-rich, high-dimensional response pattern from which we can determine the concentrations of several components in multicomponent gas mixtures encountered in practice.51 The adjustability of MOFs allows us to synthesize a diverse set of MOFs to comprise a sensor array that produces a response pattern with high information content. Inspiringly, a sensor array comprising cross-sensitive MOFs is analogous to the mammalian olfactory system (see ref 52, box 1), whose olfactory receptors are cross-sensitive to different odorants;53 in part for this reason, a sensor array in conjunction with a pattern recognition system54 is dubbed as an “electronic nose.”1,55
The computational design of MOF-based gas sensor arrays is an emerging research area pioneered by Wilmer and coworker s.52,56−59 Instead of choosing a combination of MOFs for a sensor array by trial-and-error, computational methods that can rank combinations of MOFs for a gas sensor array, based on either simulated60 or experimentally measured adsorption properties, can accelerate the development and deployment of MOF-based gas sensor arrays.
In this article, to enable the computational design of quantitative gas sensor arrays, we formulate the problem of determining the composition of a multicomponent gas mixture using mass-based MOF sensor arrays as an applied61 inverse problem.62−64 The (isothermal) forward problem associated with a set of MOFs immersed in a gas mixture is routine in the porous materials community: given the gas composition and an adsorption model, determine the mass of gas adsorbed in each MOF at thermodynamic equilibrium. The adsorption model could be constructed from experimental adsorption measurements,65,66 molecular simulations of adsorption,60 statistical mechanical models,67 or statistical machine learning models (e.g., a neural network68). The inverse problem, defined in relation to the forward problem, is encountered in gas sensing: given the mass of gas adsorbed in each MOF and an adsorption model, determine the gas composition. While the forward problem is concerned with determining the effect (gas adsorption) of the cause (gas composition), the inverse problem is concerned with determining the cause from the effect (to justify invoking causality here, we take the gas phase to be a reservoir so that the mass of gas adsorbed in the MOF cannot change the composition of the gas phase). The formulation of mass-based MOF array sensing as an inverse problem provides a methodology to rank combinations of MOFs for a gas sensor array based on their adsorption properties (more precisely, based on a mixed-gas adsorption model for each MOF in the array). When solving the inverse problem, by inverting the adsorption model, inevitable noise/error in the mass measurements propagates onto and corrupts the prediction of the gas composition; the optimal gas sensor array will yield an inverse problem whose solution, the predicted gas composition, is insensitive/robust to this measurement error.52,61
## PEDAGOGICAL INTRODUCTION TO INVERSE PROBLEMS, BY EXAMPLE
The most famous inverse problem61,69 in mathematics is perhaps “can one hear the shape of a drum? $^{,970-72}$ Here, we provide an illustrative example of an inverse problem that is more accessible to the chemical sciences.
Consider a hot cup of coffee solution at a (spatially uniform) initial temperature, $T_{0}$ . The temperature of the coffee, $T=T(t)$ , evolves with time, $t,$ as it exchanges heat with the air at ambient temperature, $T_{\mathrm{a}^{\bullet}}\:T_{0},\:T_{\mathrm{a}}\in\left(0\:{}^{\circ}\bar{\mathrm{C}},100\:{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\right)$ is taken so that the coffee begins and remains in the liquid phase. A crude dynamic model for $T=T(t)$ is, according to Newton’s law of cooling73,74
$$
C\frac{\mathrm{d}T}{\mathrm{d}t}=h A\left(T_{\mathrm{a}}-T\right)
$$
$$
T\big(t=0\big)=T_{0}
$$
N.b., model error emanating from simplifying assumptions to arrive at eq 1 also corrupts the prediction of $T_{0}$ in the inverse problem.
## FRAMING AN ELECTRONIC NOSE AS AN APPLIED INVERSE PROBLEM
We first frame quantitative gas sensing, using measurements of the mass of gas adsorbed in an array of MOFs, as a mathematical inverse problem.62−64
Consider a set of $n_{\mathrm{{m}}}$ distinct MOFs in thermodynamic equilibrium with a gas mixture containing $n_{\mathrm{g}}$ components. Let $\ensuremath{\mathbf{p}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{\mathrm{g}}}$ denote the gas compositiona vector containing the partial pressure of each component of the gas mixtureand $T$ be the temperature of the gas. Let $\mathbf{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{\mathrm{m}}}$ denote the resulting sensor array responsea vector containing the total adsorbed mass of gas in each MOF.
Adsorption Model. The adsorption model
$$
f(\mathbf{p};T)=\mathbf{m}
$$
maps the composition $\mathbf{p}$ and temperature $T$ of the gas phase to the equilibrium mass of gas adsorbed in each MOF, $\mathbf{m}$ . Hereafter, we take temperature as fixed and omit $T$ in eq 3, making $f$ the equilibrium, mixed-gas adsorption isotherm. We assume the domain of $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Sigma}}_{f}$ comprises all possible gas compositions, $\mathcal{S}\equiv[0,\infty)^{n_{\mathrm{g}}}$ . The codomain of $f$ is $\mathcal{M}\equiv[0,\infty)^{n_{\mathrm{m}}}$ , that is, $f\colon{\mathcal{P}}\to M$ .
In contrast to our previous study,52 we allow $f$ to be nonlinear to account for (i) the saturation of adsorption sites in the MOF and (ii) nonadditive contributions to the adsorbed mass of gas by different components of the gas. We take $f\colon{\mathfrak{p}}\rightarrow{\mathfrak{m}}$ to be a continuously differentiable function, thereby prohibiting adsorption/desorption hysteresis and gas-induced structural transitions seen in flexible MOFs.76
Constructing an adsorption model is routine in the porous materials community, whether through interpolating experimental adsorption data,65 ideal adsorbed solution theory (IAST),77−7 9 molecular models and simulations of gas adsorption,60 statistical mechanical models,67 or statistical machine learning models.68 N.b., a supervised machine learning model of mixed-gas adsorption, trained on adsorption data, is subject to thermodynamic constraints.80−82 The development of physics-informed neural networks that respect laws of physics is an active area of research.83
Forward Problem. The forward problem is also routine and sometimes trivialin the porous materials community: given the gas composition $\mathbf{p}$ and the adsorption model $f,$ determine the equilibrium mass of gas adsorbed in each MOF, m. As per the constraints we imposed on $f,$ which prohibit adsorption/desorption hysteresis, the solution to the forward problem exists and is unique.
(Applied) Inverse Problem. In quantitative gas sensing, we are confronted with the inverse problem, defined in relation to the forward problem: given the mass of gas adsorbed in each MOF, $\mathbf{m}_{,}$ , and the adsorption model, ${\dot{f}},$ determine the gas composition p, that is, the inverse problem is to find $\mathbf{p}$ that satisfies $\mathbf{m}=f(\mathbf{p})$ .
As opposed to an exact inverse problem, we consider the practically relevant applied inverse problem,61 where we take into account uncertainties in the inputs. In practice, the observations of the adsorbed mass of gas in each MOF are corrupted by measurement noise/error, $\delta\mathbf{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{\mathrm{m}}}$ . This measurement error will propagate onto and corrupt the prediction of the gas composition. Acknowledging this is key to evaluating the fitness of a gas sensor array: the optimal gas sensor array will yield an inverse problem whose solution, the prediction of the gas composition, is insensitive to the measurement error.
However, despite the well-posed forward problem, the applied inverse problem could have (i) multiple solutions if $f$ is not injective (one-to-one) or (ii) no solution if $f$ is not surjective (onto). An underdetermined system, giving case (i), certainly occurs if the array is composed of an insufficient number of MOFs to fully constrain the $n_{\mathrm{g}}$ degrees of freedom in the gas composition $\left(n_{\mathrm{m}}\ <\ n_{\mathrm{g}}\right)$ . Case (ii) could occur if measurement error contaminates $\mathbf{m}$ and pushes it outside the range of $f;$ the inverse problem has no solution if $\mathbf{m}+\delta\mathbf{m}\not\in M^{\prime}\equiv f(\mathcal{P})$ . This almost certainly occurs if there are more MOFs comprising the sensor array than degrees of freedom in the gas composition $\left(n_{\mathrm{m}}\ >\ n_{\mathrm{g}}\right)$ , giving an overdetermined system (see ref 52 for further discussion in the context of linear systems).
Hereafter, we assume the sensor array is composed of $n_{\mathrm{{m}}}=n_{\mathrm{{g}}}$ distinct MOFs (i) to avoid an under- or over-determined system, which will require a longer paper to treat, and (ii) as the most economical yet sufficient gas sensor array. Moreover, we take the adsorption model $f$ to be injective so that if a solution to the inverse problem exists, it is unique.
Remark on Causality. Changes in the gas composition cause changes in the amount of gas adsorbed in the MOFs, provided the gas phase is a reservoir. For this reason, adsorption models $f(\mathbf{p};T)$ are often constructed from underlying physical principles (thermodynamics) $^{60,67,77}$ but are sometimes empirical.68 While the task in the forward problem is to determine the effect $\mathbf{\tau}(\mathbf{m})$ of the cause $\bf(p)$ , in the inverse problem, the task is to determine the cause $\bf(p)$ , given knowledge of its effect $\mathbf{\tau}(\mathbf{m})$ .
Left Inverse of the Adsorption Model. The applied inverse problem has a unique solution provided $(\mathrm{i})f$ is injective and (ii) the observed mass vector falls in the range of $\scriptstyle{\hat{f}},$ that is, $\mathbf{m}+\delta\mathbf{m}\in\mathcal{M}\equiv f(\mathcal{P})$ . We find the solution by constructing the left inverse of the adsorption model, $g\colon M^{\prime}\to{\mathcal{P}}$
$$
g(\mathbf{m}){\boldsymbol{:}}=f^{-1}(\mathbf{m})=\mathbf{p}\Leftrightarrow f(\mathbf{p})=\mathbf{m}
$$
We carefully defined the domain of the left inverse $g$ to be the image of f, $\mathcal{M}^{\prime}$ . From the adsorption model $f,$ we can (i) possibly, analytically solve for $g(\mathbf{m})$ or (ii) numerically, for a given $\mathbf{m}_{i}$ , compute $\dot{g(\mathbf{m})}=\mathbf{p}$ by searching for a root84 of the function $f(\mathbf{p})$ $-\mathbf{m}$ .
Conditioning of the Applied Inverse Problem. The conditioning of an inverse problem refers to the sensitivity of its solution to a perturbation in the input.63 In an ill-conditioned (well-conditioned) problem, small perturbations in the input cause large (small) perturbations in the solution. In quantitative gas sensing, the input is the observed/measured sensor array response, and the solution is the predicted gas composition. We wish the inverse problem in gas sensing to be well-conditioned so that errors in the measured mass of adsorbed gas in each MOF minimally corrupt the predicted gas composition.
Let $\delta\mathbf{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{\mathrm{m}}}$ be a perturbation in the sensor array response, m. Assume that $(\mathrm{i})f$ is injective so that its left inverse, $g$ in eq 4, exists and (ii) the observed response falls in the image of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{j},$ that is, $\mathbf{m}+\delta\mathbf{m}\in\mathcal{M}^{\prime}$ . In the pristine inverse problem, the input is $\mathbf{m}$ , and the predicted gas composition is ${\bf p}=g({\bf m})$ . In the perturbed inverse problem, the input is $\mathbf{m}+\delta\mathbf{m},$ and the predicted gas composition is $\mathbf{p}+\delta\mathbf{p}{\stackrel{-}{=}}g(\mathbf{m}+\delta\mathbf{m}).$ , which defines $\delta\mathbf{p}$ as the perturbation of the pristine solution that results from the perturbation $\delta\mathbf{m}$ of the pristine input (see Figure 3). The conditioning of the inverse problem is characterized by the ratio of the relative error in the predicted gas composition to the error in the mass measurements (because we generally expect $\|\delta\mathbf{p}\|$ to grow with $\|\delta\mathbf{m}\|$ )
$$
\frac{\|\delta\mathbf{p}\|/\|\mathbf{p}\|}{\|\delta\mathbf{m}\|}=\frac{\|g(\mathbf{m}+\delta\mathbf{m})-g(\mathbf{m})\|/\|\mathbf{p}\|}{\|\big(\mathbf{m}+\delta\mathbf{m}\big)-\mathbf{m}\|}
$$
The equality on the right in eq 5 shows conditioning as the ratio of the relative error in the output of $g$ to the error in the input to $g$ (see Figure 3).
The condition number85 is defined by eq 5 under the limit of small $\left\|{\delta\mathbf{m}}\right\|.$ , allowing us to make a linear approximation
$$
g(\mathbf{m}+\delta\mathbf{m})\approx g(\mathbf{m})+\mathrm{J_{g}}(\mathbf{m})\delta\mathbf{m}
$$
with $\scriptstyle\mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{g}}=\mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{g}}(\mathbf{m})$ the Jacobian matrix of the left inverse function, $g$ The condition number is then
$$
\chi\colon=\operatorname*{lim}_{\varepsilon\to0^{+}}\operatorname*{sup}_{\|\delta\mathbf{m}\|\le\varepsilon}{\frac{\|\delta\mathbf{p}\|/\|\mathbf{p}\|}{\|\delta\mathbf{m}\|}}={\frac{\left\|\mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{g}}\right\|_{\mathrm{op}}}{\|\mathbf{p}\|}}
$$
and it quantifies the sensitivity of the predicted gas composition to small perturbations in the measurements of mass adsorbed in each MOF comprising the sensor array. The supremum in eq 7 is needed because, generally, $\|\delta\mathbf{p}\|$ depends on not only the magnitude of $\delta\mathbf{m}$ but also on its direction. The equality on the right of eq 7, with $\|\cdot\|_{\mathrm{op}}$ the operator norm, follows from the definition of the operator norm of Jg.
The condition number $\chi$ in eq 7 has a geometric interpretation. Under the linear approximation in eq 6, the perturbation in the gas composition is a linear transformation of the (small) perturbation of the measured masses, $\delta\mathbf{p}_{\mathbf{\alpha}}\approx$ $\mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{{g}}}(\mathbf{m})\delta\mathbf{m}$ . The operator norm of the Jacobian matrix, $\|\mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{g}}\|_{\mathrm{op}},$ is the maximum factor by which the vector $\delta\mathbf{m}$ can be stretched by transforming it with $J_{\mathrm{g}}.$ For a well-conditioned inverse problem, we wish $\|\boldsymbol{\mathrm{J_{g}}}\|_{\mathrm{op}}$ to be small to minimize the stretching of $\delta\mathbf{m}$ when it is transformed into $\delta\mathbf{p}$ by left-multiplication by $\mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{g}}.$
N.b., the condition number $\chi$ generally depends on $\mathbf{m}_{i}$ , that is, $\chi=\chi(\mathbf{m})$ because $\mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{{g}}}=\mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{{g}}}(\mathbf{m})$ . We may also say that $\chi$ depends on the gas composition to which the sensor array is exposed because $\chi(\mathbf{m})=\chi({\bar{f(\mathbf{p})}})$ .
Finally, we link the conditioning of the inverse problem directly to the properties of the adsorption model, $f,$ using the inverse function theorem. The Jacobian matrix of $g$ evaluated at m is equal to the matrix inverse of the Jacobian of f evaluated at $\mathbf{p}$ ${\bf\omega}=g({\bf m})_{\cdot}$ , giving $\chi$ viewed as a function of p
$$
\chi=\chi(\mathbf{p})=\frac{\|[\mathbf{J_{f}}(\mathbf{p})]^{-1}\|_{\mathrm{op}}}{\|\mathbf{p}\|}
$$
Because (i) the operator norm of a matrix is equal to its largest singular value and (ii) the singular values of the inverse of a matrix, if it exists, are obtained by inverting the singular values of that matrix,86 $\chi$ is equal to the inverse of the smallest singular value of $\displaystyle\mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{f}}(\mathbf{p})$ divided by $\|\mathbf{p}\|$ . Of course, the ordering of the MOFs/gases in the vectors $\mathbf{m}/\mathbf{p}$ is immaterial because the singular values of $\displaystyle\mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{f}}(\mathbf{p})$ are invariant to permutations of the rows and columns.
Evaluating the Fitness of a Gas Sensor Array. The condition number, $\chi$ in eq 7, quantifies the fitness of a proposed combination of MOFs to compose a gas sensor array. It describes the sensitivity of the predicted gas composition to errors in the measurements of the mass of gas adsorbed in the MOFs. We can determine $\chi$ from the adsorption model, $f(\mathbf{p}).$ , that governs gas adsorption in the proposed combination of MOFs via eqs 4 and 8. Because $\chi=\chi(\mathbf{m})=\chi(f(\mathbf{p}))$ , the fitness of a sensor array depends upon the gas composition to which it is exposed.
Remark on Absolute Versus Relative Errors. We can define four condition numbers based on the choice of relative or absolute error for the input and output of g.62 We opted to define the condition number in eq 7 using the relative error in the gas composition, $\|\delta\mathbf{p}\|/\|\mathbf{p}\|,$ , and the absolute error in the measured masses, $\|\delta\mathbf{m}\|$ . To justify $\|\delta\mathbf{p}\|/\|\mathbf{p}\|.$ , for example, $\lVert\delta\mathbf{p}\rVert=0.01$ bar may be acceptable if the partial pressures are on the order of
100 bar but unacceptable if on the order of 0.1 bar. The suitability of using absolute error in m depends upon the noise/ error characteristics of the (QCM or SAW) device that measures the mass of adsorbed gas in the MOFs. If the typical error $\|\delta\mathbf{m}\|$ scales with $\|\mathbf{m}\|$ , relative error is more appropriate. Our work can easily be adapted to work with relative errors in $\|\mathbf{m}\|$ . N.b., when using a condition number to compare the fitness of two sensor arrays operating in the same gas composition, defining the conditioning in terms of absolute or relative error in $\mathbf{p}$ produces equivalent rankings.
Remark on Tackling the Inverse Problem More Directly. The direction of causality favors the feasibility of directly constructing the forward function $f(\mathfrak{p})$ , then using it to find the inverse function $g(\mathbf{m})$ , as opposed to directly constructing $g(\mathbf{m})$ . However, one could directly model the inverse function via supervised machine learning techniques, 87 $g$ a common approach for using sensor arrays for classification88 (e.g. refs89−92).
Notably, in a molecular simulation, one can directly solve a related inverse problem of finding the gas composition $\mathbf{p}$ consistent with a given number of adsorbed gas particles in a single MOF, $\mathbf{n}$ (a vector, one dimension for each component of the gas). In a Monte Carlo simulation of the canonical (fixed $\mathbf{n}_{i}$ , volume, and $T$ ) ensemble of the MOF−adsorbed gas system, Widom particle insertions can be used to calculate the excess chemical potential of each adsorbed species.93 The partial pressure of each component in the gas phase in thermodynamic equilibrium with the MOF, resulting in adsorbed gas composition n, then follows. However, in the mixed-gas setting, this inverse problem is distinguished from our gas sensing problem: in the latter, we only measure the total mass of gas adsorbed in the MOF and are blind to the composition of the adsorbed phase.
## EXAMPLES OF THE ANALYSIS OF CONDITIONING OF THE INVERSE PROBLEM
We framed the problem of predicting gas composition from observation of the adsorbed masses of gas in an array of MOFs as an applied inverse problem. Now, we illustrate how this framework allows us to quantify and rank the fitness of combinations of MOFs for gas sensor arrays. For case studies, we consider single-MOF methane sensing and double-MOF methane/carbon dioxide sensing. The two candidate materials are canonical MOFs, HKUST- $\cdot1^{\mathfrak{s}_{4}}$ and IRMOF- $1,^{95}$ in which we have experimentally measured, pure- $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and pure- $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption data at $298\mathrm{K}^{66,96}$ (see Figure 4). Note that we use $\mathbf{g}/\mathbf{g}$ units for $\mathbf{m}$ , assuming an equivalent mass of each MOF is used in the array.
In the determined inverse problems we analyze below, the number of (distinct) MOFs in the array $n_{\mathrm{{m}}}$ is equal to the number of components of the gas $n_{\mathrm{g}}^{\ },$ and each observation of a mass of gas adsorbed in a MOF of the array restricts one degree of freedom in the gas composition.
The code and data to reproduce all plots in this article are available at github.com/SimonEnsemble/nonlinear_sensing.
Single-MOF Sensor Array. A single-MOF $\left(n_{\mathrm{m}}=1\right),$ ) sensor for a pure gas $\left(n_{\mathrm{g}}=1\right)$ is an instructive starting point. Consider either HKUST-1 or IRMOF-1 in thermodynamic equilibrium with a bath of pure methane gas at temperature $T$ and pressure $p$ .
Adsorption Model. We use the Langmuir adsorption model67 to describe the adsorption of methane in each MOF, at fixed temperature, as a function of pressure
$$
m=f(p;T)=m_{\infty}\frac{K p}{1+K p}
$$
Forward Problem. The (isothermal) forward problem is to predict the methane uptake in the MOF, $m$ , at a given pressure, $p$ . The solution to the forward problem is trivial: compute $m=$ $f(\boldsymbol{p})$ from eq 9 with the identified model parameters $K$ and $m_{\infty}$ .
Inverse Problem. We encounter the (isothermal) inverse problem in methane gas sensing: the task is to, given the mass of methane adsorbed in the MOF, $m$ , predict the pressure of the methane gas, $p,$ , in which the MOF is immersed. While perhaps an academic problem to use a MOF to measure the pressure of a pure gas, this $n_{\mathrm{m}}=n_{\mathrm{g}}=1$ sensing problem reveals useful insights.
Left Inverse of the Adsorption Model. To find a solution to the inverse problem for input $m\in{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime}$ , we find a closed-form expression for the left inverse of $\bar{\boldsymbol{f}}$ in eq 9
$$
p=g(m){:=}f^{-1}(m)=\frac{1}{K}\frac{m/m_{\infty}}{1-m/m_{\infty}}
$$
The domain of the inverse function $g$ : $\textit{\textbf{m}}\to\textit{\textbf{p}}$ is $\mathcal{M}^{\prime}=[0,\:m_{\infty}).$ , and its image is $\mathcal{P}=[0,\infty)$ . The left inverse $g(m)$ is shown for HKUST-1 and IRMOF-1 in Figure $5\mathrm{b}$ .
Notably, measurement error $\delta m$ could push $m+\delta m$ outside the image of $f$ so that the inverse problem has no solution, for example, if $m\:+\:\delta m\:>\:m_{\infty},$ then there does not exist a gas composition $p\geq0$ such that $f(\boldsymbol{p})=m+\delta m$ .
Conditioning of the Inverse Problem. We now study the conditioning of the inverse problem, which is solved for $m\in{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime}$ by computing $g(m)$ in eq 10.
Figure 5b geometrically illustrates how an error in the measured mass of gas, $\delta m,$ propagates onto and corrupts the predicted gas composition by $\delta p$ . In this scenario, each MOF is immersed in a bath of pure methane gas at 60 bar and $298{\mathrm{~K}}.$ More methane is adsorbed in IRMOF-1 than in HKUST-1. The measurement error $\delta m$ is equal for both single-MOF sensors. Comparing the resulting perturbation in the predicted gas composition, $\delta p$ , between HKUST-1 and IRMOF-1:
• The $\delta p$ for HKUST-1 is larger than the $\delta p$ for IRMOF-1; hence, IRMOF-1 has a higher fitness for gas sensing at $p=$ 60 bar and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ than HKUST-1 because the predicted gas composition is less sensitive to the measurement error.   
• The magnitude of $\delta p$ depends upon the direction of δm in addition to its magnitude. In the limit of small $\|\delta m\|,\|\delta p\|$ is large if $g^{\prime}(m)$ is large because then a small perturbation in $m$ results in a large perturbation in $p$ . The magnitude of $\delta p$ depends on $p$ . At lower pressures, the ranking of the MOFs switches, and HKUST-1 has a higher fitness for methane sensing than IRMOF-1.
The geometric picture in Figure $5\mathrm{b}$ reinforces the concept of conditioning and how analysis of conditioning provides a means to juxtapose the fitness of two MOFs for a gas sensor array.
The condition number in eq 7, which pertains to small $\begin{array}{r l}{\lefteqn{\|\delta m\|_{2}}}&{{}}\end{array}$ , becomes the logarithmic derivative of $\overset{\cdot}{g}$ for a single-MOF sensor
$$
\chi=\chi(m)=\frac{|g^{\prime}(m)|}{g(m)}=\frac{1}{m(1-m/m_{\infty})}
$$
and for the left inverse function in eq 10. The condition number [units: $1/(\mathrm{g/g)]}$ quantifies the perturbation of the predicted pressure of the methane gas that results from small perturbations in the measured mass of adsorbed gas in the MOF. The condition number is more useful when viewed as a function of $p$ because we aim to compare IRMOF-1 and HKUST-1 for sensing at a particular $p$
$$
\chi=\chi(p)=\frac{(1+K p)^{2}}{m_{\infty}K p}
$$
Equation 12 reveals that the inverse problem is ill-conditioned (large χ) at the two extremes of:
• low pressures $\left[\left(K p\right)\rightarrow0\Rightarrow m\rightarrow0\right]$ because the relative (not absolute) error in the predicted gas composition becomes very large at low pressures. Thus, eq 12 recovers the notion of a lower limit of detection. • high pressures $\left[\left(K p\right)\rightarrow\infty\Rightarrow m\rightarrow m_{\infty}\right]$ because the pores of the MOF saturate with gas and the mass of gas adsorbed in the MOF becomes insensitive to changes in gas composition, thereby making the predicted gas composition sensitive to errors in the measured adsorbed mass. Thus, eq 12 recovers the notion of an upper limit of detection.
low pressures for both MOFs. Moreover, the inverse problem is better-conditioned for IRMOF-1 than for HKUST-1 at pressures above ca. 12.5 bar, whereas below 12.5 bar, HKUST-1 has a lower condition number than IRMOF-1. Figure 6 therefore illustrates that the condition number $\chi$ provides a means to rank the fitness of MOFs for gas sensing at different gas compositions, given the objective is to choose a MOF that yields a prediction of the gas composition that is insensitive to the measurement error.
Finally, we link the condition number, viewed as a function of pressure, directly to the adsorption model $f(\boldsymbol{p})$ through eq 8
$$
\chi=\chi(p)={\frac{1}{p!f^{\prime}(p)!}}
$$
Two-MOF Sensor Array. We now consider a gas sensor array of $n_{\mathrm{{m}}}=2$ MOFs, IRMOF-1 and HKUST-1, tasked with determining the partial pressures of the $n_{\mathrm{g}}=2$ components in a methane/carbon dioxide mixture.
Consider HKUST-1 and IRMOF-1 in thermodynamic equilibrium with a $\mathrm{CH_{4}}/\mathrm{CO_{2}}$ mixture at temperature $T$ with partial pressures $\begin{array}{r l r}{{\bf p}}&{{}=}&{\left[p_{\mathrm{CO_{2}}},p_{\mathrm{CH_{4}}}\right]\in\mathbb{R}^{2}}\end{array}$ . Let $\begin{array}{r}{\begin{array}{r c l}{\mathbf{m}}&{=}&{\left[m_{\mathrm{HKUST-1}},\right.}\end{array}}\end{array}$ $m_{\mathrm{IRMOF-1}}]\in\mathbb{R}^{2}$ be the total mass adsorbed in each MOF, comprising both $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ gas.
Adsorption Model. As a mixed-gas $\left(\mathrm{CH_{4}}/\mathrm{CO_{2}}\right)$ adsorption model for each MOF, we invoke IAST,77−79 a thermodynamic framework for predicting mixed-gas adsorption from purecomponent (pure $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{.}$ ) adsorption isotherms. As input, IAST requires a mathematical model for the purecomponent adsorption isotherms in Figure 4c,d. For the pure$\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption isotherms, we use the fitted Langmuir models (see eqn 9) shown in Figure 5a. We also use a fitted Langmuir model for the pure- $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm in HKUST-1. However, we resort to smoothed cubic spline interpolation of the pure- $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption data in IRMOF-1 because the Langmuir model cannot capture the inflection in the data. Figure S1 shows all four pure-component adsorption isotherm models on top of the experimental data. The IAST framework, together with the pure-component adsorption models, provide our mixed-gas adsorption model, $\mathbf{m}\ ={\bar{\boldsymbol{f}}}(\mathbf{p};\ T).$ , though not explicitly.
Forward Problem. The (isothermal) forward problem is to predict the total mass of gas adsorbed, comprising both $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ in each MOF, $\mathbf{m}$ , given the partial pressures of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ in the gas phase, p.
We find the solution to the forward problem through IAST calculations, implemented in pyIAST78; that is, pyIAST provides a numerical implementation of the function ${\bf m}=f({\bf p})$ .
We visualize the IAST-based adsorption model as a mapping $f\colon{\mathfrak{p}}\rightarrow{\mathfrak{m}}$ in Figure 7a.52,97 The thick, black boundary shows how the square in gas composition space (left) is nonlinearly transformed into the more complicated shape in sensor response space (right). Moreover, the vertical/horizontal lines in composition space are transformed into the same-colored vertical-ish/horizontal-ish lines in response space. The images of the vertical lines in composition space have a smaller arc length in response space than the images of the horizontal lines. This indicates that the mass of gas adsorbed in the MOFs is less sensitive to changes in the partial pressure of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ than to changes in the partial pressure of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ ; generally $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ has a higher affinity for MOFs than $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ owing to its polar bonds. We also can see how the small square regions in composition space, formed by the lines, are mapped into response space. As the partial pressures of the components increase, either together or independently, the images of the (same size) squares in composition space occupy less area in response space. This is because, as the pores of the MOF fill with gas, the mass of gas adsorbed in the MOF becomes less sensitive to the charges in the partial pressure.
Inverse Problem. We encounter the (isothermal) inverse problem in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ gas sensing: the task is to, given the total mass of adsorbed gas in each MOF, ${\bf m},$ predict the partial pressures of the two components, p, in the gas phase in which the MOFs are immersed.
Left Inverse of the Adsorption Model. To implement the left inverse of the adsorption model, $g(\mathbf{m})=\mathbf{p}$ , which produces the solution to the inverse problem (if a solution exists), we resort to a numerical root finding algorithm $\left(\mathrm{in\SciPy}^{98}\right.$ )
$$
{\bf p}=g({\bf m})=\mathrm{search~for~}{\bf p}\mathrm{~such~that~}{\bf m}-f({\bf p})=0
$$
N.b., sensor array responses that fall outside the gray region in Figure 7a are outside the image of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{j},$ in that case, a solution to the inverse problem does not exist.
We visualize the mapping g: $\mathbf{m}\rightarrow\mathbf{p}$ in Figure $76$ in a similar manner as in Figure 7a. The most important feature of the mapping is that the (same size) squares in response space are stretched into larger regions in composition space by $g$ as the MOFs adsorb more gas. As a consequence, the inverse problem will be ill-conditioned when the pores of the MOF are nearly saturated with gas; small errors in the measured masses will be stretched into large errors in composition space. Figure 8a illustrates this consequence more clearly.
Figure 8a shows how circles in response space are mapped into composition space. In response space, the x’s represent pristine measurements of $\mathbf{m}$ , and the circles around the $x^{\prime}s$ represent perturbed measurements, contaminated by errors $\delta\mathbf{m}$ of the same magnitude. In gas composition space, the samecolored $x$ represents the true gas composition, with the surrounding oval outline representing the corrupted predictions of the gas composition from using the observations $\mathbf{m}+\delta\mathbf{m}$ to solve the inverse problem. Each colored region in response space represents an uncertainty zone associated with the marked $x$ : owing to measurement errors, we are unable to confidently distinguish the response m marked with $x$ from all responses that fall in its uncertainty zone. As a result, we can at best confidently conclude that the gas composition falls somewhere within the cognate, colored zone in composition space. Figure 8a reveals that as a result of measurement errors of fixed magnitude $\|\delta\mathbf{m}\|_{\partial}$ , the perturbation of the predicted gas composition, $\delta\mathbf{p}.$ ,
• depending on the direction of $\delta\mathbf{m},$ could have a much larger component in the direction of $p_{\mathrm{CH_{4}}}$ because the mass of gas adsorbed is less sensitive to changes in $p_{\mathrm{CH}_{4}},$ compared to $p_{\mathrm{CO}_{2}}$ . Consequently, the condition number $\chi$ in eq 7 has a dominant contribution from the error in the predicted $p_{\mathrm{CH_{4}}}$ . tends to increase in magnitude as the partial pressures of the gases increase (together or separately). This is a result of the pores of the MOF saturating with gas, causing adsorption to be less sensitive to charges in partial pressure.
Conditioning of the Inverse Problem. Figure 8b displays the condition number associated with the inverse problem, given in eq 8 and viewed as a function of gas composition, that is, $\chi=$ $\chi(\mathbf{p})$ . We computed $\chi(\mathfrak{p})$ via (i) numerical differentiation (finite difference method implemented in numdifftools in Python) of $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Sigma}}_{f}$ to obtain the Jacobian $\displaystyle\mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{f}}(\mathbf{p})$ , (ii) computation of the smallest singular value of $\displaystyle\mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{f}}(\mathbf{p})$ , $\sigma_{2},$ and then (iii) computation of the operator norm $\|[\bar{\mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{f}}(\mathbf{p})}]^{-1}\|_{\mathrm{op}}=\left.\sigma_{2}^{-1}\right.$ . As in the 1D case, the inverse problem is ill-conditioned at low partial pressures (representing the lower limits of detection) and at higher partial pressures of $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ when the pores of the MOFs saturate with gas. The latter reinforces our observations in Figure 8a, which shows that measurement errors $\delta\mathbf{m}$ can be stretched into large $\delta\mathbf{p}$ when the partial pressure of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is high.
Gas Sensor Arrays Cope with Cross-Sensitivity. In gas sensing using QCMs or SAWs, only the total mass of gas is adsorbed, which, for cross-sensitive MOFs, has a contribution from each component of the gas phase. With an adsorption model, however, we have the luxury of showing the adsorption of each component of the gas. Figure S2 shows the mass of each component, $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ of the $\mathrm{CH_{4}}/\mathrm{CO_{2}}$ mixture adsorbed in each HKUST-1 and IRMOF-1, as a function of the gas composition, according to IAST. Indeed, HKUST-1 and IRMOF-1 are cross-sensitive MOFs; when exposed to a $\mathrm{CH}_{4}/$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ mixture, each appreciably adsorb both components of the gas. Therefore, observation of the total mass of gas adsorbed in either HKUST-1 or IRMOF-1 in isolation is insufficient to predict the composition of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ mixture because, for example, a measurement of $0.05~\mathrm{g/g}$ could be due to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . The total mass of gas adsorbed in both HKUST-1 and IRMOF-1 together, $\mathbf{m}_{,}$ , however, is sufficient to arrive at a unique prediction of the gas composition, as shown in Figure 7b. This underscores the necessity for gas sensor arrays in practical gas sensing applications, where the gas composition has multiple degrees of freedom. Sensor arrays allow us to overcome crosssensitivity of any individual MOF by allowing each MOF to constrain one degree of freedom in the gas phase; collectively, the responses of the MOFs pinpoint the gas composition.
## DISCUSSION
We framed quantitative gas sensor arrays, where measurements of the mass of gas adsorbed in an array of NPMs are used to infer the composition of a gas, as a mathematical inverse problem. The fitness of a proposed combination of NPMs for quantitative gas sensing is determined by the conditioning of its inverse problem. Conditioning refers to the robustness/insensitivity of the predicted gas composition to errors in the measurements of the masses of adsorbed gas in the NPMs. We demonstrated how the condition number allows us to evaluate and compare the fitness of combinations of NPMs for gas sensor arrays using a single- and double-MOF sensor array for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ sensing. MOFs are a particularly attractive class of NPMs for a gas sensor array because they are tunable14 and can be grown into homogeneous, thin films with controlled orientation and thickness for miniature devices.99 We find that the conditioning of the inverse problem depends not only on the adsorption properties of the MOFs composing the array but also on the gas composition. In general, the inverse problem becomes illconditioned (i, low detection limit) at low pressures because the relative error in the predicted gas composition blows up and (ii, high detection limit) at high pressures, when the pores of the adsorbent become saturated with gas, causing the mass of gas adsorbed in the MOFs to be insensitive to changes in gas composition. Though we illustrated conditioning analysis with a one- and two-MOF sensor array for a one- and two-component gas, respectively, the ideas in this article generalize to sensor arrays composed of $n_{\mathrm{m}}~>~2$ for $n_{\mathrm{g}}~=~n_{\mathrm{m}}$ gases that yield determined inverse problems.
In addition to the mass of adsorbed gas, other measurable properties of NPMs that depend on the gas composition can be exploited to sense gases, for example, luminescence,26 mechanical strain,27,2 8 or conductivity.30 The concept in this article, namely to quantify the fitness of a combination of NPMs for a gas sensor array using the condition number $\chi,$ also applies to these other sensing mechanisms, provided that one can construct a model analogous to eq 3 that relates the measured property of the NPM (the analogy to m) to the gas composition. Exploiting the physical phenomena that “the adsorbed mass of gas in a NPM depends upon the gas composition” to construct a gas sensor is attractive from a computational standpoint because we can, using molecular models and simulations, readily predict the amount of gas adsorbed in a NPM as a function of gas composition and temperature.60 This enables high-throughput computational screenings of NPM-based electronic noses that exploit the adsorbed mass of gas in the NPM.56 Moreover, the NIST database of experimentally measured gas adsorption isotherms in NPMs100,101 can be used to construct adsorption models and screen combinations of NPMs for gas sensor arrays. However, caution is warranted when aggregating experimental measurements from different laboratories owing to, possibly, (unintentional) biases in measurements among different laboratories.102
Computationally ranking combinations of NPMs for gas sensor arrays is an emerging research area. $^{52,56-59}$ At this juncture, we highlight shortcomings of our work and provide new directions.
First, we examined the gas sensing problem under the (restrictive) conditions of thermodynamic equilibrium, without considering the dynamics of gas adsorption into the MOF crystallites/thin films, arising from surface barriers and/or intracrystalline diffusion limitations.103−107 The dynamics of gas adsorption into the MOF pose both challenges and opportunities for gas sensing. Challenges include, if the inference of the gas composition is based on the equilibrium sensor response, long waiting times and blindness to transient, rapidly varying inputs. On the other hand, opportunities lie in exploiting differences in gas adsorption dynamics among different gas species to extract information from the dynamics of the response of the sensorinformation complementary to the equilibrium response. That is, including the time-dependency of the sensor response as an input to the inverse problem could potentially improve the predictions of the gas composition.
Our gas sensing framework requires a priori knowledge of what constituents are possibly present in the gas phase. If gas species $x$ is present in the gas phase and adsorbs appreciably in the MOF but is not accounted for in the adsorption model ${\dot{f}}(\mathbf{p})$ in eq 3, the prediction of the gas composition using the adsorption model is invalid. Fundamentally, we cannot foresee how to circumvent this, except to, for all gas species that could be present with reasonable probability, incorporate them into the adsorption model.
We studied how measurement errors corrupt the prediction of the gas composition, but we ignored model error that emanates from the simplifying assumptions to arrive at the equilibrium gas adsorption model ${\bf{m}}=f({\bf{p}})$ . For example, in this work, we took (i) $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption to obey a Langmuir model with the identified $K$ and $m_{\infty}$ and (ii) mixed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption to follow IAST, with pure-component adsorption isotherms modeled by interpolation and the Langmuir model, respectively. The underlying assumptions of the single-site Langmuir adsorption model67 in eq 9 are (i) the material offers $m_{\infty}$ identical, independent adsorption sites and (ii) adsorbed gas molecules do not interact. $\mathrm{LA}\dot{\mathrm{S}}\mathrm{T}^{79}$ assumes that (i) the adsorbed phase forms an ideal mixture, (ii) each adsorbed species can access the same pores in the MOF, and (iii) gas-induced structural changes do not occur.78 Extensions to IAST108,109 relax these assumptions. The gold standard but laborious and expensive metric of the fitness of a gas sensor array is its generalization error on unseen gas compositions, through repeated experiments of: (a) exposing the sensor array to a known gas composition not used in the procedure to construct the inverse function $g(\mathbf{m})$ , (b) observe the sensor response, (c) use $g$ to predict the gas composition, and (d) compare the predicted composition with the true composition. A crossvalidation procedure can provide a reliable estimate of this generalization error.
While we took isothermal conditions in our article, we can adapt our modeling framework to handle varying temperatures by constructing a temperature-dependent adsorption model f(p; T) as in eq 3, then either (i) input the measured temperature of the gas, identified by an independent measurement, into the adsorption model or (ii) include the temperature as an additional variable to be determined in the inverse problem.
We defined the condition number χ in eq 7 using the absolute error in the measurement, but the expected magnitude of the measurement error could be dependent on the magnitude of the measurement. The noise characteristics of the mass measurement device (QCM or SAW) should be further characterized before sensor design and deployment. For future work, we aim to specify a probabilistic distribution for the errors in the measured masses, $\delta\mathbf{m}$ , then determine the distribution of the error in the predicted gas composition, $\delta\mathbf{p}$ , that results when solving the inverse problem.
Finally, we aim to study ill-posed inverse problems for future work. In the underdetermined problem, for example, when there are fewer MOF sensors than gases $(n_{\mathrm{m}}<n_{\mathrm{g}})$ , the sensor response is insufficient to fully constrain the gas composition, but (i) it could still provide useful, though incomplete, information about gas composition and (ii) introducing regularization or incorporating a prior belief would alter the inverse problem and give it a unique solution. In the overdetermined problem, when there are more MOF sensors than gases $(n_{\mathrm{m}}>n_{\mathrm{g}})$ , we can cast the inverse problem as a least squares problem, as in ref 52, giving it a unique solution.
